# g03 -- the row reaper cuts a chain only when no snapshot needs it.
#
# T1 reads R1 at its snapshot, holding the watermark back. T2 updates
# R1 twice and commits. T3's read of R1 queues the key for the row
# reaper, whose pass, run by T2 with no transaction, reaps nothing:
# CURR is not settled, and T1 still reads 0 through the chain. Once T1
# ends, a read queues R1 again, and a pass reaps it; every reader then
# sees T2's last value (../../GC.md §13.5).


== pairing RR/RR == T1=RR T2=RR T3=RC
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@2 T1 read R1 -> 0
@3 T2 begin -> ok
@3 T2 write R1 a -> ok
@3 T2 commit -> ok
@4 T2 begin -> ok
@4 T2 write R1 b -> ok
@4 T2 commit -> ok
@5 T3 begin -> ok
@5 T3 read R1 -> b
@5 T3 commit -> ok
@6 T2 reap -> 0
@7 check last T2 0 -> satisfied
@8 T1 read R1 -> 0
@9 check last T1 0 -> satisfied
@10 T1 commit -> ok
@11 T3 begin -> ok
@11 T3 read R1 -> b
@11 T3 commit -> ok
@12 T2 reap -> 1
@13 check last T2 1 -> satisfied
@14 T3 begin -> ok
@14 T3 read R1 -> b
@14 T3 commit -> ok
@15 T2 reap -> 0
@16 check last T2 0 -> satisfied
@17 check row R1 b -> satisfied
